  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"PowerToys#27790\", \"PowerToys#11233\", \"PowerToys#30310\"], \"answer\": \"This has been requested before. Awake can't yet turn itself on and off on a schedule, such as 09:00–18:00 on chosen weekdays. The maintainers are tracking scheduling requests in PowerToys#24846 (referenced from #27790), so add your use case or a thumbs-up there. Awake can already stop at a set date and time (\\\"until expiration\\\"), but you still have to start it yourself. Until scheduling exists, you can use Windows Task Scheduler. Create a task that launches PowerToys.Awake.exe at the start of your workday, for example on workdays or when the PC resumes from sleep. Pass it --expire-at=18:00:00 or --time-limit. Users in #27790 and #11233 describe this workaround. One user's script also stops any running Awake instance first. Results with it have been mixed: one user reported the options were ignored and Awake ran indefinitely.\"}",
